    hermesDeepDive: {
      heading: "What Is Hermes Agent & Why Does It Excel at Tool Use?",
      paragraphs: [
        "Developed by Nous Research, Hermes Agent (built upon Hermes 3 and Llama 3 architectures) is an open-weights model specifically fine-tuned for agentic workflows.",
        "Standard chat LLMs often struggle when asked to execute function calls or produce structured JSON schemas. They might output invalid syntax, hallucinate function arguments, or miss tool definitions.",
        "Hermes Agent was trained explicitly on multi-step reasoning, JSON Schema validation, and tool-call orchestration. When presented with custom function definitions, Hermes accurately generates structured `<tool_call>` outputs that can be parsed and executed in local sandboxes."
      ]
    },
    techNotes: {
      heading: "Technical Deep Dive: Under the Hood of Local Execution",
      intro: "Architectural components that make local AI agents fast, secure, and reliable.",
      notes: [
        {
          title: "1. Quantization & Hardware Memory Math (GGUF & AWQ)",
          before: "Full 16-bit Float (FP16):  70B parameters ≈ 140 GB VRAM (Requires datacenter GPU)\n4-bit Quantized (Q4_K_M):  70B parameters ≈ 40 GB VRAM  (Fits on dual RTX 3090/4090)\n4-bit Quantized (Q4_K_M):  8B parameters  ≈ 5.5 GB VRAM (Runs easily on standard laptop)",
          beforeLabel: "VRAM requirements by quantization precision:",
          after: null,
          afterLabel: null,
          extraCode: null,
          extraLabel: null,
          note: "Quantization techniques like GGUF compress weight precision from 16-bit floats to 4-bit integers with minimal accuracy loss. This allows developers to run 8B to 70B parameter models on consumer hardware."
        },
        {
          title: "2. The Agentic Tool-Use Execution Loop",
          before: "User Prompt → Hermes System Prompt (<tools> JSON Schema )\n                  ↓\n   Model generates <tool_call> {\"name\": \"query_db\", \"args\": {...}} </tool_call>\n                  ↓\n   Local Runtime intercepts & executes function in local sandbox\n                  ↓\n   Tool Result fed back into context → Hermes generates final response",
          beforeLabel: "Hermes Agent tool-execution orchestration sequence:",
          after: null,
          afterLabel: null,
          extraCode: null,
          extraLabel: null,
          note: "Because the execution loop runs locally, the tool execution has direct access to local Vector Databases (ChromaDB, Qdrant), local filesystems, or internal microservices securely."
        },
        {
          title: "3. Local OpenAI-Compatible Server Implementation",
          before: "// Local Node.js / Full Stack fetch pointing to local Ollama / vLLM endpoint\nconst response = await fetch('http://localhost:11434/v1/chat/completions', {\n  method: 'POST',\n  headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },\n  body: JSON.stringify({\n    model: 'hermes3:8b',\n    messages: [\n      { role: 'system', content: 'You are a secure local Full Stack assistant.' },\n      { role: 'user', content: 'Analyze local system telemetry.' }\n    ],\n    tools: [{\n      type: 'function',\n      function: {\n        name: 'get_system_metrics',\n        description: 'Fetch local CPU and memory usage',\n        parameters: { type: 'object', properties: {} }\n      }\n    }],\n    temperature: 0.2\n  })\n});\nconst data = await response.json();",
          beforeLabel: "Connecting Full Stack applications to local Hermes endpoints:",
          after: null,
          afterLabel: null,
          extraCode: null,
          extraLabel: null,
          note: "Local inference engines (Ollama, llama.cpp, vLLM) expose identical REST API interfaces to cloud providers, making switching between cloud and local inference seamless."
        }
      ]
    },
    remember: {
      heading: "Things I Want To Remember",
      items: [
        "Local AI ensures 100% data privacy — confidential client data never leaves local hardware.",
